(
"The cross-host differential's case table in INTERSECTION SOURCE: each case is DEF(name, S2(expr, operand)) and every host reduces expr applied to operand, printing name=result for the harness to compare byte-for-byte. The cases pin the semantics of record (python/delta.py): selectors and their range edge, the sequence prims and their bottom edges, NATEQ, coercion in arithmetic and comparison (int-first then float, lexical only for non-numeric string pairs), the controlling forms including the WHILE countdown, and transpose. Fuel cases stay host-side (fuel is a host policy, not canon).",

DEF("case:sel2", S2(N(2), S3(A("x"), A("y"), A("z")))),
DEF("case:sel-range", S2(N(5), S2(A("x"), A("y")))),
DEF("case:tl", S2(A("tl"), S3(A("x"), A("y"), A("z")))),
DEF("case:tl-atom", S2(A("tl"), A("atomic"))),
DEF("case:atom-t", S2(A("atom"), A("x"))),
DEF("case:atom-f", S2(A("atom"), S2(A("x"), A("y")))),
DEF("case:null-t", S2(A("null"), PHI())),
DEF("case:null-f", S2(A("null"), S1(A("x")))),
DEF("case:eq-nateq", S2(A("eq"), S2(N(1), A("1")))),
DEF("case:eq-same", S2(A("eq"), S2(A("a"), A("a")))),
DEF("case:add-int", S2(A("+"), S2(N(2), N(3)))),
DEF("case:add-lex", S2(A("+"), S2(A("120"), A("30")))),
DEF("case:add-bad", S2(A("+"), S2(A("a"), A("3")))),
DEF("case:mul-lex", S2(A("*"), S2(A("4"), N(5)))),
DEF("case:lt-coerce", S2(A("lt"), S2(A("9"), A("10")))),
DEF("case:lt-mixed", S2(A("lt"), S2(N(4997), A("11000")))),
DEF("case:ge-multi", S2(A("ge"), S2(A("305"), A("1190")))),
DEF("case:lt-words", S2(A("lt"), S2(A("apple"), A("pear")))),
DEF("case:apndl", S2(A("apndl"), S2(A("h"), S2(A("a"), A("b"))))),
DEF("case:apndr", S2(A("apndr"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), A("t")))),
DEF("case:distl", S2(A("distl"), S2(A("k"), S2(A("p"), A("q"))))),
DEF("case:distr", S2(A("distr"), S2(S2(A("p"), A("q")), A("k")))),
DEF("case:cat", S2(A("cat"), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S1(A("c"))))),
DEF("case:reverse", S2(A("reverse"), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
"or had no case, which is how a base op could be absent from four stations unnoticed. Backus 11.2.3 lists and, or, not together; and and not were cased and or was not.",
DEF("case:or-ft", S2(A("or"), S2(A("F"), A("T")))),
DEF("case:or-ff", S2(A("or"), S2(A("F"), A("F")))),
DEF("case:or-tt", S2(A("or"), S2(A("T"), A("T")))),
DEF("case:or-nonbool", S2(A("or"), S2(A("x"), A("y")))),
DEF("case:rotl", S2(A("rotl"), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
DEF("case:rotr", S2(A("rotr"), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
DEF("case:oner", S2(A("1r"), S3(N(1), N(2), N(3)))),
DEF("case:tlr-phi", S2(A("tlr"), PHI())),
DEF("case:trans", S2(A("trans"), S3(S2(N(1), N(2)), S2(N(3), N(4)), S2(N(5), N(6))))),
DEF("case:trans-ragged", S2(A("trans"), S2(S2(N(1), N(2)), S1(N(3))))),
DEF("case:div", S2(A("/"), S2(N(4), N(2)))),
DEF("case:div-zero", S2(A("/"), S2(N(4), N(0)))),
DEF("case:comp", S2(S3(A("COMP"), A("tl"), A("tl")), S3(A("a"), A("b"), A("c")))),
DEF("case:cons", S2(S3(A("CONS"), N(2), N(1)), S2(A("p"), A("q")))),
DEF("case:const", S2(S2(A("CONST"), A("k")), A("ignored"))),
DEF("case:cond", S2(S4(A("COND"), A("null"), S2(A("CONST"), A("empty")), S2(A("CONST"), A("full"))), PHI())),
DEF("case:alpha", S2(S2(A("ALPHA"), A("tl")), S2(S2(A("a"), A("b")), S2(A("c"), A("d"))))),
DEF("case:insert-max", S2(S2(A("INSERT"), A("system:max2")), S4(A("2"), A("19"), A("7"), A("4")))),
DEF("case:while-count", S2(S3(A("WHILE"), S3(A("COMP"), A("gt"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S2(A("CONST"), N(0)))), S3(A("COMP"), A("-"), S3(A("CONS"), A("id"), S2(A("CONST"), N(1))))), N(5))),
DEF("case:bu", S2(S3(A("BU"), A("+"), N(10)), N(7))),
DEF("case:const-bottom", S2(S3(A("COMP"), S2(A("CONST"), A("k")), N(1)), PHI())),
"Codd 1970 1.3 via arest's own ruling (the rmap:schema note): the flat table is the DERIVED PRESENTATION rmap:unnest, not a stored form. engine/rust computes that projection in 442 lines of Rust (op_sql_project) with NO test anywhere, so the canon path that ought to replace it had no oracle on either lineage. These two give it one. The first is a single role level; the second nests a level inside a level, which is the curry (one store level per role) and is where unnest recurses through rmap:unnest_cell -- the recursion the DEFS lookup resolves, so no host owes a loop.",
DEF("case:rmap-unnest", S2(A("rmap:unnest"),
    S2(S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(A("x"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Bob"), S1(A("y")))))),
DEF("case:rmap-unnest-curried", S2(A("rmap:unnest"),
    S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(S3(A("CELL"), A("age"), S1(A("42")))))))),
"store:namerows against engine/rust's `cells` op: unsorted input, one multi-row cell and one single-row cell, so the case pins BOTH the counting and the sort. The third cell is an ATOM cell -- no population -- and must answer bottom, which is the `null` rows field engine/rust emits.",
DEF("case:store-namerows", S2(A("store:namerows"),
    S3(S3(A("CELL"), A("Bob"), S2(A("x"), A("y"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Ada"), S1(A("z"))),
       S3(A("CELL"), A("Cyd"), A("atom"))))),
"rmap:wide_row is the 3NF ENTITY-TABLE PIVOT -- id, then one slot per absorbed column -- which engine/rust rebuilds in op_sql_project (the `rows.push(row)` loop over per-column maps). The second case is the one that matters and is why this could not simply be transliterated: engine/rust writes SQL NULL as bottom INSIDE the row (`unwrap_or_else(bot)`), which its Vec<V> tolerates but Backus 13.2 does not -- a sequence containing bottom IS bottom, so that row would be no row at all. Canon answers an ABSENT slot with phi and a present one with a non-empty slot (rmap:lookup0), the option encoding, and the host renders phi as null. Same boundary store:namerows hit. The -mixed case is the one that proves it usable: one present column and one absent column answer <'a',<<'x'>>,phi> -- ARITY PRESERVED, one slot per column, which is what a table row has to be. The -null case alone could not show that, since a single absent column and a ragged row look alike from one row.",
DEF("case:rmap-wide-row", S2(A("rmap:wide_row"),
    S2(A("a"),
       S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))))))),
DEF("case:rmap-wide-row-null", S2(A("rmap:wide_row"),
    S2(A("zz"),
       S1(S5(A("F1"), S2(A("Thing"), A("Widget")), S1(S1(N(1))), PHI(),
             S1(S2(A("a"), A("x")))))))),
"rmap:ddl_table against the two forms engine/rust emits (main.rs 10827, 10949). The entity form carries its key on a column's own type and passes phi keycols; the relation form passes the key columns and gets the trailing clause. The second case also carries a REFERENCES column, which is the arm the Rust splits in two and canon does not.",
"rmap:ddl_name's two arms. The guard case is the real defect ddl._sql_name records: 'SQLite Fact Base' slugs to sqlite_fact_base, which SQLite refuses as a reserved namespace, so it must come back t_sqlite_fact_base. The plain case must NOT be prefixed -- a guard that fires on everything would pass a test that only checked the guard.",
"rmap:ddl_order over all three ways a table can be ready -- no parents at all (parent), a parent that is not a projected table (orphan/external), and a self-reference (self) -- with one table (child) that must WAIT for its parent. Ready tables leave sorted per wave, so wave 1 is orphan,parent,self and child follows: a globally sorted answer would put child first and is the wrong answer. The cycle case is the terminating arm: a and b each wait on the other, nothing is ever ready, and the remainder goes out sorted instead of looping forever.",
"rmap:coldisamb: the FIRST 'a' keeps its name and the later ones become a_2, a_3 -- suffixing every duplicate including the first would also produce distinct names and would be the wrong answer, so the case pins which one keeps the base. 'b' and 'c' occur once and must come back untouched.",
"rmap:colsof over rmapColumns rows given OUT of column order and with another table's row mixed in. The answer must be ftA,ftC -- ftA is written last but numbered 2, so a version that kept cell order would answer ftC,ftA and the DDL's columns would stop matching the rows. The 'other' row must not appear at all.",
"rmap:rolesof with the roles given out of order and another fact type's role mixed in. The tie case pins what canon does when two roles share a position -- engine/rust sorted the (pos,player) tuple, canon orders by position alone, and a position is unique within its fact type so the store never presents this; pinned rather than assumed.",
"rmap:rolegroups: F1 must come before F2 because F1's role is seen first, even though F2 sorts earlier -- first-seen order, not alphabetical, is what the entity sweep walks.",
"rmap:refmode's two precedences, each with the name present TWICE so a uniform rule cannot pass. Person is in refScheme twice: LAST wins (nr, not code). Car is in refMode twice and absent from refScheme: FIRST wins (vin, not plate). Van is in neither: id. Person also appears in refMode, which must be ignored entirely -- refScheme having spoken settles it.",
"rmap:entities keeps only the ObjectType rows. The -short case is the one worth having: a one-element row must be SKIPPED, exactly as engine/rust's it.len() >= 2 skips it. Without the length guard 2 of that row is bottom and the whole sweep answers bottom instead of losing one row -- a failure that looks nothing like the one row it came from.",
"rmap:sortnames over bare atoms -- the case exists because system:sort_asc answers bottom on them, which is what ord_names hit. owntables drops the absorbed fact type and sorts what is left (F2 is written before F1 in the rows, so an unsorted answer shows). entitytables keeps every entity and adds the absorbing tables that are not own fact types -- F2 is own, so it must NOT appear.",
"rmap:playerpos keeps only the roles the table itself plays: F1 names Person at position 1 and Car at 2, F2 names only Car, so Person answers <F1,1> alone. rmap:atpos selects position 2 dynamically through apply and SKIPS the short row -- without the guard that row is bottom and the whole projection is bottom, not one value short.",
"rmap:valpairs drops the row that cannot carry both an id and a value, and keeps ROW ORDER -- the duplicate id is emitted twice on purpose, because the last-wins rule lives in the host's map (keyed by its own value identity) and canon must not pre-empt it by silently keeping one.",
"colfts and coltables over the same rmapColumns rows: ftA is absorbed by two different tables and person absorbs two fact types, so both projections must dedup. Order is dedup's last-occurrence and is pinned rather than relied on -- one answer feeds a setminus exclusion, the other a set.",
"The rule split: r2 is an aggregate so it must land in the agg list and NOT the plain one, and the one-element row r3 must land in NEITHER -- a row without a head cell names no derivation. Both lists come from the same fold over the same rows, so a case that only checked the plain list would miss an aggregate leaking into the closure, which is the exact failure the split exists to prevent (an aggregate head supersedes rather than unions).",
DEF("case:aggids", S2(A("derive:aggids"),
    S2(S2(A("r2"), A("count")), S1(A("r5"))))),
DEF("case:rules-plain", S2(A("derive:rules_plain"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
DEF("case:rules-agg", S2(A("derive:rules_agg"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
"derive:rulesplit must answer <plain, agg> -- the SAME two lists the separate folds give, from one pass. Pinned beside them so the one-pass form cannot drift from the two-pass one it replaced.",
DEF("case:rulesplit", S2(A("derive:rulesplit"),
    S2(S1(A("r2")),
       S3(S2(A("r1"), A("H1")), S2(A("r2"), A("H2")), S1(A("r3")))))),
DEF("case:colfts", S2(A("rmap:colfts"),
    S3(S3(A("person"), N(2), A("ftA")),
       S3(A("person"), N(3), A("ftC")),
       S3(A("car"), N(2), A("ftA"))))),
DEF("case:coltables", S2(A("rmap:coltables"),
    S3(S3(A("person"), N(2), A("ftA")),
       S3(A("person"), N(3), A("ftC")),
       S3(A("car"), N(2), A("ftA"))))),
"rmap:pivot_row over one row present and one absent, with all three cell shapes in each: a unary column, a functional column carrying the SAME id twice (so the LAST value must win -- z, not x), and a functional column that has nothing for this id (phi, the SQL NULL). The -absent case takes an id in none of them and must still answer a row of the SAME ARITY -- 0 and two phis, never a short row, because a table row has one cell per column.",
"rmap:take of more than a sequence holds must answer the whole sequence, not bottom -- the -over case. rmap:takerows SKIPS the row too short to bind its roles (engine/rust's `narrow`) and truncates the one that is too long, so the case carries one of each beside a row that already fits: a version that padded instead of skipping would answer three rows where two is right.",
"theta:firstseen beside theta:dedup on the SAME input, because the difference is the whole point. On <a,b,a,c,b>: dedup answers <a,c,b> -- each value's LAST occurrence, in the POSITION order of those last occurrences (a at 3, c at 4, b at 5) -- and firstseen answers <a,b,c>. The prose here first claimed dedup gave <b,c,a>; the case said otherwise, which is why both are pinned rather than one described. engine/rust's id sweep keeps the FIRST id it meets, so only firstseen is the rule it needs.",
"theta:aton against engine/rust's num(). The round trip is the point: aton of ntoa's answer must be the number back. '007' pins that leading zeros are digits, not a format. '-42' pins the sign arm. The three REFUSALS are what keep this honest -- '1.5' is a float and must come back as TEXT (canon does not imitate a platform float parser, the same rule that stopped set_key moving), '12a' is not a digit run, and the bare '-' has no magnitude after the sign and must not answer 0.",
"theta:groupby1: r1 appears twice with different values and must gather BOTH, in row order; r2 comes second because its first row is second, not because it sorts later. The one-element row is skipped, not padded -- and it sits in the middle so a version that stopped at the first bad row would lose r1's second value too.",
"derive:ftr_pairs swaps each role row to <ft, roleid> and keeps the FIRST of any duplicate. The repeat of <r1,F1> must collapse to one pair, and the two rows sharing F1 with DIFFERENT role ids must both survive -- deduping on the ft alone would lose the second, which is a fact type losing a role.",
"derive:uniq_constraints keeps BOTH uniqueness kinds and drops everything else. The mandatory row must not survive -- a restrict that matched any constraint would pass a case carrying only uniqueness rows -- and the two-element row is skipped rather than answering bottom for the whole restrict.",
"derive:copy_pairs takes elements 2 and 3, NOT 1 and 2 -- the row's first field is the rule id and is not part of the inclusion. The two-element row is skipped rather than answering bottom. The repeated pair is emitted TWICE on purpose: the caller collects these into a set, and collapsing them here would pre-empt a decision that call site has already made.",
"derive:txn_unambiguous carries all three rules at once: t1 is defined twice in the SAME machine and survives (a repeat is not a conflict); t2 is defined in TWO machines and is excluded WHOLE, not resolved to either; and the row already carrying the txn: surrogate is skipped, because an earlier pass keyed it and it must not be re-derived. Dropping any one rule still passes a case testing only the others. The surrogate here reads txn:M9-t9, a stand-in, because nothing in THIS case tests the separator -- only the txn: prefix. The separator itself is cased under derive:txn_surrogate now that it can be: no escape for a control character is legal in all five lineages (\\xHH is rejected by java, \\uHHHH by rust, both established by compiling them), so canon writes \\xHH and the java composer rewrites it to java's own \\u00HH spelling.",
"derive:reads_hit across all four ways it can answer: an intersection in the MIDDLE of the read set (not the first cell, which a test checking only the head would pass), no intersection at all, an EMPTY frontier, and a rule that READS NOTHING. The last two must both answer F -- an empty frontier waking every rule, or a rule with no reads waking on any change, are the two ways a wrong answer here would silently re-run the whole closure.",
"derive:atom_hits keeps the atoms whose FACT TYPE (element 2) is in the delta, not those whose kind matches -- the pair is <kind, fact type> and testing the wrong half is the mistake available here. ORDER is preserved: p1 before p4, as the rule declares them, because the join applies one ~d variant per hit in that order. The empty-delta case answers phi rather than every atom.",
DEF("case:atom-hits", S2(A("derive:atom_hits"),
    S2(S2(A("F1"), A("F3")),
       S4(S2(A("p1"), A("F1")), S2(A("p2"), A("F2")),
          S2(A("p3"), A("F9")), S2(A("p4"), A("F3")))))),
DEF("case:atom-hits-empty", S2(A("derive:atom_hits"),
    S2(PHI(), S2(S2(A("p1"), A("F1")), S2(A("p2"), A("F2")))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S2(A("A"), A("B")), S3(A("X"), A("B"), A("Y"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-miss", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S1(A("A")), S2(A("X"), A("Y"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-nofrontier", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(PHI(), S1(A("X"))))),
DEF("case:reads-hit-noreads", S2(A("derive:reads_hit"),
    S2(S1(A("A")), PHI()))),
DEF("case:txn-unambiguous", S2(A("derive:txn_unambiguous"),
    S5(S2(A("t1"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t2"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t1"), A("M1")),
       S2(A("t2"), A("M2")),
       S2(A("txn:M9-t9"), A("M9"))))),
DEF("case:copy-pairs", S2(A("derive:copy_pairs"),
    S4(S3(A("r1"), A("A"), A("B")), S2(A("r2"), A("C")),
       S3(A("r3"), A("D"), A("E")), S3(A("r4"), A("A"), A("B"))))),
DEF("case:uniq-constraints", S2(A("derive:uniq_constraints"),
    S4(S3(A("c1"), A("uniqueness"), A("F1")),
       S3(A("c2"), A("mandatory"), A("F2")),
       S3(A("c3"), A("spanning_uniqueness"), A("F3")),
       S2(A("c4"), A("uniqueness"))))),
DEF("case:ftr-pairs", S2(A("derive:ftr_pairs"),
    S4(S2(A("r1"), A("F1")), S2(A("r2"), A("F2")),
       S2(A("r1"), A("F1")), S2(A("r3"), A("F1"))))),
"theta:groupby1_rest over THREE-wide rows, which is the case groupby1 cannot express: each group holds the whole rest of its row, <kind, cell>, not one value. The existing groupby1 case runs beside it unchanged, which is what says the specialisation still means what it meant when it was its own fold.",
DEF("case:groupby1-rest", S2(A("theta:groupby1_rest"),
    S4(S3(A("r1"), A("pos"), A("A")), S3(A("r2"), A("neg"), A("B")),
       S1(A("short")), S3(A("r1"), A("pos"), A("C"))))),
DEF("case:groupby1", S2(A("theta:groupby1"),
    S4(S2(A("r1"), A("A")), S2(A("r2"), A("B")), S1(A("short")), S2(A("r1"), A("C"))))),
DEF("case:aton", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("120"))),
DEF("case:aton-zeros", S2(A("theta:aton"), A("007"))),
